#
#       Reversed, because the console echoes the command before the machine
#       answers it: a marker written plainly here is in the transcript whether
#       or not anything ran, which is a check that passes when the thing it
#       watches is broken. Every other answer in this file is reversed for the
#       same reason.
#
#       Nothing configured, so awk runs its command as gawk would; the same
#       launch after root refuses awk's spawn does not.
#
printf 'fl-free-%s\n' "$(awk 'BEGIN { system("printf out") }' 2>/dev/null | rev)"
printf 'fl-named-%s\n' "$(env /bin/dash -c 'printf ready' 2>/dev/null | rev)"
#
#       A live register refusing nothing leaves the shell's process
#       semantics as bash has them: after a structural fork, with a child of
#       its own still running, an explicit exec replaces it in place, where a
#       supervising shell refuses while it has children. And /proc masked
#       by a tmpfs changes nothing a launch no row restricts.
